## General Style Guidelines

- Use 2 spaces for tabs
- Maximum line length of 80 characters
- Add newline at end of every file
- Use dangling commas for multi-line lists
- Keep braces on same line (with noted exceptions)
- Always use if-else structure when all paths return
- Property initialization should be in declaration when possible

## TypeScript Best Practices

- Always include type annotations (except for arrow functions)
- Use enums instead of union types for fixed values
- Use strict type checking
- Avoid the `any` type; use `unknown` when type is uncertain
- Always add TSDoc (except for injection properties)
- Use Maybe<T> and Nullable<T> for optional/nullable properties
- Use MaybePromise<T> for functions that return T | Promise<T>
- Align TSDoc text and follow proper tag ordering

## Angular Best Practices

- Components can be standalone or part of NgModules
- Use standalone components for simpler organization
- Implement lazy loading for feature routes
- Follow consistent file structure
- Use proper TypeScript access modifiers: never use `public` (it's default); use `private` and `protected` appropriately
- Keep change detection optimized

## Import Guidelines

Use aliased imports (@bydesign/*) when importing from: outside current project, outside current root directory, subdirectories, sibling subdirectories.

Use relative imports only for: files in same directory, files directly above in same root directory.

## Components

- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility
- Use `@Input()` and `@Output()` decorators for component communication
- Set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` in `@Component` decorator
- Use BehaviorSubject/Observable pattern for reactive state
- Use proper naming conventions (*.component.ts)
- Prefer Reactive forms over Template-driven forms
- Avoid complex logic in templates
- Keep component state immutable
- Use resolvers for "core" data loading
- Initialize in constructor when possible, use ngOnInit only when necessary

Property organization order (with newlines between groups): ViewChilds → Inputs → Outputs → Public (excluding injections) → Protected → Private → Public injections → Protected injections → Private injections.

## State Management

- Use BehaviorSubject for local component state
- Expose state as Observable using .asObservable()
- Name observables with $ suffix
- Keep state transformations pure and predictable
- Use proper RxJS operators for state transformations

## Templates

- Keep templates simple and avoid complex logic
- Use structural directives (*ngIf, *ngFor, *ngSwitch)
- Always use the async pipe with observables
- Use trackBy with *ngFor for better performance
- Use proper typing for template variables
- Break complex templates into smaller components

## Services & Extensions

- Design services around a single responsibility
- Use proper dependency injection hierarchy
- Use inject() function instead of constructor injection
- Document public API methods with TSDoc
- Handle errors appropriately
- Use TypeScript interfaces for service responses
- Extensions don't follow singularization convention; extension files are suffixed with ".extensions"; extension classes are suffixed with "Extensions"

```ts
export class MyService {
  private readonly httpClient = inject(HttpClient);
  private readonly errorHandler = inject(ErrorHandler);
  protected readonly config = inject(ConfigService);
}
```

## Store Patterns

- Use Store base class for state management
- Implement getters that throw when state doesn't exist
- Use withState function for state updates
- Use afterSync(state) for post-sync operations
- Prefer stores over singleton services in standalone components
